{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792764163,"nonce":"G7g3skMyyQ27c2EFprSzNhnOEkA=","title":"Native CLI startup overrides preset tier policy and optimizes the whole program before main","message":"At 75069892e50b64a7f962a99ac6c9d002aefc138a, resolve_tier_config in src/main.rs sets config.start_interpreted=false for every preset when the project has no explicit [tier] section. This includes Script and Application, whose preset definitions request interpreter-first startup. Consequently fast_interpreter_start in run_file is false and the CLI runs the full O2 MIR pipeline before execution. Baseline native compilation then compiles all retained functions synchronously in initialize_loaded_modules. The backend uses CraneliftBackend::with_symbols (speed), although OptimizationTier::Baseline declares none as its intended optimization level.\n\nCurrent startup audit: 12 CI kernels, three fresh processes per default/native and eager-LLVM mode, fresh project-cache directories with the embedded stdlib snapshot enabled. First-main marker is inserted in temporary source copies. Default cold-to-main medians range from 73 to 349 ms; cache-hit processes range from 25 to 54 ms. DeltaBlue medians: 295 ms first main, 165 ms in upfront MIR optimization; a single warm-cache process reached main in 37 ms. Machine CPU contention caused wide wall-time ranges; retain raw ranges rather than treating these as release performance guarantees.\n\nThe pass profiler identifies InsertFree, loop unswitching, loop unrolling, inlining, LICM and repeated whole-module optimizer rounds as substantial costs. Reducing the opt level is not currently a safe blanket workaround: native O0/O1 fails fannkuchredux and increases the execution portion of several other kernels sharply. Fix the separate interpreter/minimal-MIR correctness blockers, establish a valid mandatory fast pipeline, honor presets, and move expensive optimization to actual hot-function promotion.\n\nHotness currently does not make that transition happen for these CLI runs: record_call is reached from execute_function but native direct calls do not increment counters; src/main.rs disables OSR probes. Every default sweep records one entry call, no Beadie routes or installs, and all compiled functions at Baseline. True per-function baseline compilation, stable call stubs and native hotness/OSR are needed so reducing startup work does not permanently sacrifice hot performance. Standard/Optimized Beadie compilation also builds a fresh backend over all modules, so one promoted root can repeat unrelated compilation work.\n\nThe current jit phase timer measures only compile_module registration. It excludes backend construction, initialize_loaded_modules (where baseline compilation occurs), and the eager LLVM upgrade, so its microsecond values hide native startup work. Add explicit process-to-main, backend setup, baseline compilation/finalization, static init, and promotion timings; distinguish throughput/warmup benchmarks from source startup measurements.\n\nEvidence: /tmp/rayzor-startup-audit/summary.csv, summary.json, results.json, optimization-sweep/results.json and *.events.json. Related ongoing investigations: b59090a08270af602d9d91ea4bd5e344833666aa68a3e49e9f4be2cab3543cd3 and 6cab7d6c85aee9b77c6aca341d5a592f10131571f01ea0f5a14403248a668d79. No repository source changes were made.","files":null}]}